{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018KLzVw3j2NnjzbawjxWUwU","type":"tool_result","content":"370\t`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n371\t`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\n372\town record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n373\t\n374\t**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n375\t(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\n376\tnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n377\t`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n378\t\n379\t**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\n380\ttwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\n381\twhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\n382\tare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\n383\tthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\n384\tfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\n385\tcatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\n386\thappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n387\t\n388\t**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\n389\tgame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, a Home Run Duel that reuses the Derby rules (§6.18): each side's best slugger takes 5\n390\tswings against the Pitch-O-Matic, and the most home-run feet wins (a tie goes on one swing each). The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\n391\tinnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n392\t\n393\t**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\n394\tappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\n395\twalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n396\t\n397\t**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\n398\tfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\n399\tpitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\n400\tchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n401\t\n402\t**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\n403\touts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\n404\tbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n405\t\n406\t### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n407\t\n408\t**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\n409\tslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\n410\tsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n411\t\n412\t**Delivery, for a human:**\n413\t1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard 1–5, or K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button\n414\t   throws). The\n415\t   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n416\t2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n417\t   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n418\t3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n419\t   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n420\t   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n421\t   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n422\t4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n423\t   × the pitcher's BRK factor × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n424\t\n425\t**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\n426\tits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n427\t\n428\t**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n429\t/ speed:\n430\t- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n431\t- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n432\t- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n433\t\n434\tSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n435\t(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's VEL factor\n436\t(§7) × (1 + 0.08 · q) × the stamina factor × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n437\t\n438\t**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\n439\tThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\n440\tthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n441\t\n442\t**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n443\t`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\n444\tbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\n445\treliever.\n446\t\n447\t**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\n448\twild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's GLV / 10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n449\t\n450\t**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\n451\tmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n452\t\n453\t**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\n454\tsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\n455\tedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n456\t\n457\t### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n458\t\n459\t**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n460\t±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\n461\tr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's CON factor (§7: 0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\n462\tthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n463\t\n464\t**Swings:**\n465\t\n466\t| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n467\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n468\t| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n469\t| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n470\t| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n471\t| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n472\t\n473\t**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n474\t|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\n475\td > 1.6 r. The timing grades are **PERFECT** within ±22 ms, **GOOD** within ±50 ms and **OK** within ±90 ms (all ×\n476\t`TUNE.swingTiming` × assist). Otherwise:\n477\t- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n478\t  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n479\t- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n480\t- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n481\t  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n482\t  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n483\t- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n484\t  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n485\t- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × POW factor (0.88–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n486\t  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n487\t- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n488\t  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n489\t  hook to the pull side.\n490\t- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n491\t  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n492\t\n493\t**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\n494\tpitch crosses.\n495\t\n496\t**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\n497\tmirrored mocap swing.\n498\t\n499\t**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\n500\tgrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\n501\tBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\n502\tmapping).\n503\t\n504\t### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n505\t\n506\t**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n507\t- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n508\t- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n509\t- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n510\t- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n511\t\n512\t**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n513\t`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n514\t260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n515\t\n516\t**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n517\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n518\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n519\t- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n520\t\n521\t**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\n522\tsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n523\t\n524\t**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\n525\tthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\n526\tsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n527\t\n528\t**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\n529\tleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\n530\tland 30–60 U wide.\n531\t\n532\t**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n533\t(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\n534\tThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n535\t\n536\t**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\n537\troll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\n538\texactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n539\t\n540\t### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n541\t\n542\t**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\n543\tlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\n544\tfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\n545\tintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\n546\tto the next best.\n547\t\n548\t**Everyone else covers by role:**\n549\t- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n550\t- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n551\t- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n552\t- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n553\t- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n554\t- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n555\t  the outfielder.\n556\t\n557\t**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × SPD factor × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\n558\ttime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n559\t\n560\t**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × GLV factor × `TUNE.catchRadius`\n561\tacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\n562\tin a wall climb. Success:\n563\t`p = clamp(0.985 + 0.006 · (GLV − 5) − speedPenalty − 0.08 if diving − difficultyPenalty, 0.5, 0.995)`, where the speed\n564\tpenalty is 0.05–0.15 for balls above 600 U/s.\n565\tA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\n566\tdizzy-stars gag.\n567\t\n568\t**Throwing:**\n569\t- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard 1, 2, 3, 4, or L, I, J, K; mouse:\n570\t  click the base in the world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × ARM factor\n571\t  (0.88–1.15) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n572\t- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n573\t- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n574\t  presses the base again.\n575\t- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n576\t  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n577\t  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n578\t  play).\n579\t\n580\t**Outs on the bases:**\n581\t- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n582\t- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n583\t  swipe animation.\n584\t- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n585\t  they throw when the runner turns.\n586\t\n587\t**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n588\t**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n589\t18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n590\t\n591\t**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\n592\titself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n593\t\n594\t**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\n595\trange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\n596\ttries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\n597\tbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n598\t\n599\t### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n600\t\n601\t**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × SPD factor (0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\n602\trunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n603\t`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n604\t- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n605\t- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n606\t- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n607\t  (tag up) before advancing.\n608\t\n609\t**Orders (human):**\n610\t- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n611\t- L1 / Q: send all back.\n612\t- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n613\t  keyboard 1–4, or L, I, J, K).\n614\t- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n615\t\n616\t**Steals and leads:**\n617\t- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n618\t- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n619\t- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n620\t- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n621\t\n622\t**Slides:**\n623\t- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n624\t  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n625\t- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n626\t\n627\t**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls POW + 1d6\n628\t(`rng.play`) against the catcher's POW + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\n629\tcatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n630\t\n631\t**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n632\t\n633\t**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n634\t\n635\t**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\n636\ttag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\n637\tan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\n638\tpickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n639\t\n640\t### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n641\t\n642\t**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\n643\tfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n644\t\n645\t| Event | Gain |\n646\t|---|---|\n647\t| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n648\t| single | +10 |\n649\t| extra-base hit | +15 |\n650\t| home run | +25 |\n651\t| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n652\t| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n653\t| double play | +15 |\n654\t| stolen base | +10 |\n655\t| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n656\t| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n657\t| a park target hit | +20 |\n658\t\n659\tAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n660\t\n661\t**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n662\t\n663\t| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n664\t|---|---|---|---|\n665\t| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n666\t| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n667\t| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n668\t| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n669\t\n670\tThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\n671\tinnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n672\t\n673\t**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\n674\tfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\n675\tmeter clamps at 100).\n676\t\n677\t### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n678\t\n679\t**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n680\t10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\n681\tThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\n682\tanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n683\t\n684\t### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n685\t\n686\t**Triggers:**\n687\t- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n688\t- a bowl-over (0.25);\n689\t- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n690\t- the dev trigger.\n691\t\n692\tSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n693\t\n694\t**Flow:**\n695\t1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n696\t2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n697\t   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n698\t3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n699\t   defense); the rest are AI.\n700\t4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n701\t5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n702\t   is really ejected.\n703\t6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n704\t\n705\t**Fighting:**\n706\t- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n707\t  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n708\t- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n709\t- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n710\t- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n711\t  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n712\t\n713\t**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n714\t\n715\t**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\n716\ton first after; no stray state after it ends).\n717\t\n718\t### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n719\t\n720\tEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\n721\trate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\n722\tline the first time it happens.\n723\t\n724\t| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n725\t|---|---|---|\n726\t| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n727\t| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n728\t| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n729\t| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n730\t| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n731\t| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n732\t\n733\t**Wind** (the four open-air parks; the two domes have none): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\n734\tballs above 60 U. A windsock (a shape) and the banners' sway show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n735\t\n736\t**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\n737\tthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n738\t\n739\t### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n740\t\n741\tFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n742\t\n743\t| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n744\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n745\t| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n746\t| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n747\t| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n748\t| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n749\t| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n750\t| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n751\t| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n752\t| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n753\t\n754\tThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n755\t\n756\t- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n757\t  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n758\t  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n759\t  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n760\t- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n761\t  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n762\t- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n763\t  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n764\t- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n765\t  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n766\t  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n767\t\n768\t**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over seeded 9-inning games at Pro (6 games in the default run, 30 with `--full`;\n769\tsims run with `M.fast = true`: no walk-ups, shows, replays or cinematics, and the CPU decides at once): runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\n770\tplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\n771\tover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\n772\tones).\n773\t\n774\t### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n775\t\n776\t**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n777\t`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n778\t- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n779\t- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n780\t- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n781\t  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n782\t  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n783\t  a super).\n784\t- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n785\t- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n786\t- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n787\t  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n788\t- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n789\t\n790\t**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n791\t(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n792\t`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n793\t- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n794\t- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n795\t\n796\t**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n797\t- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n798\t- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n799\t- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n800\t\n801\tZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\n802\tthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n803\t\n804\t**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n805\t1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n806\t2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n807\t   point);\n808\t3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n809\t   skippable with ✕).\n810\t\n811\tThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n812\t\n813\t**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\n814\trig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\n815\tthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\n816\tcorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n817\t\n818\t**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n819\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a605ca8e-6f21-4c44-bd74-b97a2b38e121","uuid":"80b37171-388b-400b-a4bb-7804c63140fa","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:01:25.049Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\nown record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n\n**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\nnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n\n**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\ntwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\nwhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\nare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\nthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\nfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\ncatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\nhappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n\n**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\ngame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, a Home Run Duel that reuses the Derby rules (§6.18): each side's best slugger takes 5\nswings against the Pitch-O-Matic, and the most home-run feet wins (a tie goes on one swing each). The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\ninnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n\n**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\nappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\nwalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n\n**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\nfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\npitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\nchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n\n**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\nouts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\nbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n\n### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n\n**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\nslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\nsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n\n**Delivery, for a human:**\n1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard 1–5, or K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button\n   throws). The\n   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n   × the pitcher's BRK factor × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n\n**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\nits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n\n**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n/ speed:\n- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n\nSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's VEL factor\n(§7) × (1 + 0.08 · q) × the stamina factor × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n\n**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\nThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\nthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n\n**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\nbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\nreliever.\n\n**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\nwild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's GLV / 10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n\n**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\nmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n\n**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\nsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\nedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n\n### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n\n**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\nr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's CON factor (§7: 0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\nthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n\n**Swings:**\n\n| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n\n**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\nd > 1.6 r. The timing grades are **PERFECT** within ±22 ms, **GOOD** within ±50 ms and **OK** within ±90 ms (all ×\n`TUNE.swingTiming` × assist). Otherwise:\n- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × POW factor (0.88–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n  hook to the pull side.\n- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n\n**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\npitch crosses.\n\n**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\nmirrored mocap swing.\n\n**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\ngrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\nBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\nmapping).\n\n### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n\n**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n\n**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n\n**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n\n**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\nsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n\n**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\nthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\nsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n\n**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\nleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\nland 30–60 U wide.\n\n**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\nThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n\n**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\nroll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\nexactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n\n### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n\n**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\nlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\nfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\nintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\nto the next best.\n\n**Everyone else covers by role:**\n- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n  the outfielder.\n\n**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × SPD factor × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\ntime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n\n**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × GLV factor × `TUNE.catchRadius`\nacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\nin a wall climb. Success:\n`p = clamp(0.985 + 0.006 · (GLV − 5) − speedPenalty − 0.08 if diving − difficultyPenalty, 0.5, 0.995)`, where the speed\npenalty is 0.05–0.15 for balls above 600 U/s.\nA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\ndizzy-stars gag.\n\n**Throwing:**\n- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard 1, 2, 3, 4, or L, I, J, K; mouse:\n  click the base in the world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × ARM factor\n  (0.88–1.15) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n  presses the base again.\n- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n  play).\n\n**Outs on the bases:**\n- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n  swipe animation.\n- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n  they throw when the runner turns.\n\n**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n\n**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\nitself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n\n**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\nrange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\ntries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\nbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n\n### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n\n**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × SPD factor (0.88–1.15) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\nrunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n  (tag up) before advancing.\n\n**Orders (human):**\n- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n- L1 / Q: send all back.\n- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n  keyboard 1–4, or L, I, J, K).\n- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n\n**Steals and leads:**\n- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n\n**Slides:**\n- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n\n**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls POW + 1d6\n(`rng.play`) against the catcher's POW + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\ncatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n\n**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n\n**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n\n**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\ntag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\nan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\npickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n\n### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n\n**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\nfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n\n| Event | Gain |\n|---|---|\n| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n| single | +10 |\n| extra-base hit | +15 |\n| home run | +25 |\n| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n| double play | +15 |\n| stolen base | +10 |\n| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n| a park target hit | +20 |\n\nAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n\n**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n\n| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n\nThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\ninnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n\n**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\nfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\nmeter clamps at 100).\n\n### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n\n**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\nThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\nanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n\n### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n\n**Triggers:**\n- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n- a bowl-over (0.25);\n- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n- the dev trigger.\n\nSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n\n**Flow:**\n1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n   defense); the rest are AI.\n4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n   is really ejected.\n6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n\n**Fighting:**\n- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n\n**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n\n**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\non first after; no stray state after it ends).\n\n### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n\nEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\nrate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\nline the first time it happens.\n\n| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n\n**Wind** (the four open-air parks; the two domes have none): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\nballs above 60 U. A windsock (a shape) and the banners' sway show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n\n**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\nthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n\n### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n\nFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n\n| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n\nThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n\n- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n\n**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over seeded 9-inning games at Pro (6 games in the default run, 30 with `--full`;\nsims run with `M.fast = true`: no walk-ups, shows, replays or cinematics, and the CPU decides at once): runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\nplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\nover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\nones).\n\n### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n\n**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n  a super).\n- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n\n**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n\n**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n\nZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\nthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n\n**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n   point);\n3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n   skippable with ✕).\n\nThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n\n**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\nrig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\nthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\ncorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n\n**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n","numLines":450,"startLine":370,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018KLzVw3j2NnjzbawjxWUwU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
