{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghvyNDverhqqQzTx6VDo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ApLao1XAw5E2dUgL2BKNK9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":820,"limit":560},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14945,"cache_read_input_tokens":37434,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14945},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a605ca8e-6f21-4c44-bd74-b97a2b38e121","uuid":"0354bcfc-4d20-43e8-85d0-f33c80b40ea0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:01:29.251Z","request_id":"req_011CghvyMyJRLscTiktWdfCd","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01ApLao1XAw5E2dUgL2BKNK9":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","offset":820,"limit":560}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ApLao1XAw5E2dUgL2BKNK9","type":"tool_result","content":"820\t**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\n821\tthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\n822\tscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\n823\trestored).\n824\t\n825\t### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n826\t\n827\t**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n828\t`game.cornerArea()`:\n829\t- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n830\t  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n831\t- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n832\t  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n833\t- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n834\t  pitcher's stamina bar.\n835\t- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n836\t  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n837\t- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n838\t  advance).\n839\t- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n840\t  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n841\t- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n842\t- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n843\t  the ball.\n844\t- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n845\t  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n846\t- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n847\t  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n848\t\n849\t**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\n850\tTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\n851\ttoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n852\t\n853\t**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\n854\treturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n855\t- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n856\t  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n857\t- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n858\t- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n859\t- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n860\t- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n861\t- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n862\t- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n863\t\n864\t**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n865\t- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n866\t  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n867\t- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n868\t\n869\t**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\n870\tfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\n871\tthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n872\t\n873\t### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n874\t\n875\t**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\n876\tbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\n877\tThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. On the keyboard, the number keys and PC-standard keys come first (below), and\n878\tthe I J K L cluster is an alternate set laid out like the face buttons.\n879\t\n880\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n881\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n882\t| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | Space, K | left button |\n883\t| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n884\t| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n885\t| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n886\t| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q, Tab | – |\n887\t| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n888\t| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n889\t| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n890\t| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n891\t| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n892\t| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n893\t| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n894\t| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n895\t| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n896\t| `num1`–`num5` | (face buttons do these jobs) | | 1 2 3 4 5 | – |\n897\t| pitch type | face buttons | | 1–5 (or K L J I, E) | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n898\t\n899\t**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n900\t\n901\t| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n902\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n903\t| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n904\t| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n905\t| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n906\t| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n907\t| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n908\t| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n909\t| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n910\t\n911\t- **The keyboard is PC-native first:** WASD and the mouse aim and run; Space swings and confirms; the mouse's left\n912\t  button swings, throws at a clicked base and selects; the right button is the power swing; **number keys pick**\n913\t  (`num1`–`num5`: 1–5 the pitch type when pitching, 1–4 the base, first to home, wherever a base is chosen); Shift\n914\t  is turbo (sprint); Tab switches fielder; E is the super and \"go\"; Q is the steal or \"back\" modifier; C or Ctrl\n915\t  dives; Escape pauses. The I J K L cluster stays as an alternate set that mirrors the face buttons, for players who\n916\t  like a pad layout on keys. Keyboard prompts show the number keys first.\n917\t- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n918\t  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n919\t  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n920\t- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n921\t  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n922\t  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n923\t  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n924\t- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n925\t  use.\n926\t- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n927\t- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n928\t  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n929\t- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n930\t  setting).\n931\t\n932\t**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n933\t(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel and the number keys\n934\tpick pitch types, and the number keys pick bases; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\n935\tof its contexts' prompts).\n936\t\n937\t### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n938\t\n939\t**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\n940\tslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\n941\tgusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n942\t\n943\t**The commentary director:**\n944\t- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n945\t- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n946\t  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n947\t- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n948\t- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n949\t- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n950\t- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n951\t  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n952\t- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n953\t\n954\t**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n955\t- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n956\t- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n957\t  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n958\t- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n959\t- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n960\t- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n961\t\n962\t**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n963\t- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n964\t  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n965\t- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n966\t  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n967\t    offset from a hash.\n968\t  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n969\t  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n970\t    10-ms noise burst.\n971\t- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n972\t  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n973\t- **Voice profiles:**\n974\t\n975\t| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n976\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n977\t| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n978\t| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n979\t| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n980\t| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n981\t| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n982\t| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |\n983\t| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n984\t| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n985\t| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n986\t| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n987\t\n988\t- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n989\t- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n990\t\n991\t**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n992\t`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n993\t`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n994\t✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n995\t\n996\t**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\n997\tno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n998\t\n999\t### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n1000\t\n1001\t**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\n1002\tno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\n1003\tapart with a slow vibrato.\n1004\t\n1005\t| Song | Mood and makeup |\n1006\t|---|---|\n1007\t| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n1008\t| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n1009\t| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n1010\t| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n1011\t| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n1012\t| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n1013\t| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n1014\t| `training` | light and bouncy |\n1015\t| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n1016\t| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n1017\t\n1018\t**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\n1019\tvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n1020\t\n1021\t**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n1022\t- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n1023\t- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n1024\t- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n1025\t- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n1026\t- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n1027\t- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n1028\t- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n1029\t- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n1030\t  engine's `explode`.\n1031\t- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n1032\t- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n1033\t- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n1034\t\n1035\t**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\n1036\twarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n1037\t\n1038\t### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n1039\t\n1040\t- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n1041\t  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n1042\t- **Behavior:**\n1043\t  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n1044\t  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n1045\t  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n1046\t  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n1047\t- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n1048\t  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n1049\t- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n1050\t  the between-innings shows.\n1051\t- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n1052\t- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n1053\t  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n1054\t  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n1055\t  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n1056\t  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n1057\t- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n1058\t\n1059\t**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\n1060\thand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n1061\t\n1062\t### 6.18 Game modes\n1063\t\n1064\t- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n1065\t  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n1066\t  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n1067\t    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n1068\t  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n1069\t    reward item and the next pin.\n1070\t  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n1071\t- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n1072\t  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n1073\t- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n1074\t  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n1075\t  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n1076\t  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n1077\t    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n1078\t  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n1079\t  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n1080\t  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n1081\t- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n1082\t  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n1083\t  be replayed.\n1084\t\n1085\t### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n1086\t\n1087\t- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n1088\t  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n1089\t- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n1090\t  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n1091\t- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n1092\t  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n1093\t- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n1094\t  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n1095\t  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n1096\t    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n1097\t  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n1098\t  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n1099\t\n1100\t### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n1101\t\n1102\t- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n1103\t  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n1104\t- **Sides:**\n1105\t  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n1106\t  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n1107\t  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n1108\t- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n1109\t  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n1110\t  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n1111\t  - With one human, they do everything.\n1112\t- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n1113\t- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n1114\t  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n1115\t  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n1116\t- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n1117\t\n1118\t**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\n1119\tplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n1120\t\n1121\t### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n1122\t\n1123\t**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\n1124\tsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\n1125\ttheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\n1126\tGhosts never affect your round.\n1127\t\n1128\tWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n1129\t(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\n1130\tgame stays local (§6.20).\n1131\t\n1132\t**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n1133\t```html\n1134\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n1135\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n1136\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1137\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1138\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n1139\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n1140\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n1141\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/Space/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n1142\t```\n1143\tThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n1144\t`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n1145\t\n1146\t**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1147\t1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n1148\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n1149\t   normally to the title.\n1150\t3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n1151\t   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n1152\t4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n1153\t   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n1154\t   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n1155\t     check;\n1156\t   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n1157\t   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n1158\t   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n1159\t     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n1160\t   \n1161\t   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n1162\t   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n1163\t   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n1164\t5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n1165\t   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()` (just 120000 when `limitMs` is missing or 0).\n1166\t6. **Reporting:**\n1167\t   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n1168\t     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n1169\t   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n1170\t     **once**.\n1171\t   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n1172\t7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n1173\t   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n1174\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n1175\t     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n1176\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n1177\t   \n1178\t   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n1179\t8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n1180\t   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n1181\t   are canvas text, never HTML.\n1182\t\n1183\t**Session rules:**\n1184\t- **No title or restart:** Quit to title, Restart, Dev Mode and Pitching Change are hidden, and the back gesture is\n1185\t  swallowed.\n1186\t- **No popups or sockets:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, new windows or sockets.\n1187\t- **Storage:** the client already gives an empty storage, so add nothing for it.\n1188\t- **Pause:** the pause menu offers only Resume, Settings and Controls, and the round clock keeps running.\n1189\t\n1190\t**Tests:**\n1191\t- Headless T-HUB-01..08 use a mock `AIHub` in the vm context: connect, ready without input, the card's answers, start\n1192\t  at time 0, progress every second, a single result, time-up, end stopping input and sound, ghost messages at ≤ 20/s\n1193\t  and applied without changing the local score, and `Math.random` not called during the card.\n1194\t- Browser: the real harness in Playwright (§11.7).\n1195\t\n1196\t### 6.22 Attract mode (`35-attract.js`)\n1197\t\n1198\t- **Starts** after **10 s with no input** on the title (its mode menu included) or the other top-level menus (the Tour\n1199\t  menu, Team Select before anyone joins, Derby Setup, the lesson list, About). Input means any key, button, click,\n1200\t  touch, stick past the dead zone, or mouse movement over 4 px.\n1201\t- **Exits** on any input: back to the title (input consumed, so it doesn't pick a menu item); the idle timer restarts.\n1202\t- **What it is:** a looping **highlight reel played by the real game**, run by a director over `game.debug` with\n1203\t  cheats on (forced pitches and contact, infinite meter, no outs).\n1204\t- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" banner and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the icon for the device used last; on\n1205\t  touch-only devices \"TOUCH TO RETURN\"), plus a caption card between segments.\n1206\t- **Segments** (§7.11) cover the Plate Cam duel, super pitches, fielding, a diving catch, a Laser Arm throw, a steal and\n1207\t  slide, a bowl-over, a Batzooka moonshot with fireworks, a Rhubarb, low-gravity wall climbs, the Phantom Ball, the\n1208\t  drones and boss, the Derby with ghost comets, and the Moonshot Leaders table. One loop takes about 2.5 minutes.\n1209\t- **Robustness:** each segment has a time limit and moves on by itself if its event doesn't happen.\n1210\t- **Never** in an AI Hub session.\n1211\t\n1212\t**Tests:** T-ATT-01..05 (idle 10 s on the title starts it; it doesn't start during play or with the dev panel open;\n1213\tevery segment runs in order without errors and the reel loops; any input returns to the title; touch-only mode still\n1214\truns it).\n1215\t\n1216\t### 6.23 Dev mode (`36-dev.js`)\n1217\t\n1218\t**Reached** from the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (any pausable scene; hidden in hub sessions). It is a large,\n1219\t10-foot-readable panel over the frozen game, with six tabs. L1/R1 or Q/E change tabs; up and down move; left and right\n1220\tchange a value (hold to accelerate); ✕ toggles or activates; △ resets this value (or pins a slider); ○ closes. The\n1221\tmouse hovers, clicks, and drags sliders.\n1222\t\n1223\t1. **TUNE**: every `BZ.TUNE` value as a **live slider** grouped as in Appendix A (Pitching, Batting, Ball, Players,\n1224\t   Over the top, CPU, Camera, Game). Values apply instantly.\n1225\t   - **Reset to defaults** for all, and a reset per group.\n1226\t   - \"Keep tuning between visits\" stores the values in `E.store('bz:tune')`.\n1227\t   - **Pinned sliders:** △ pins up to 3. While playing, a thin strip at the top shows them; hold Create (Pad8) with\n1228\t     the D-pad to adjust (keyboard: `[` and `]` adjust, `\\` picks the next pin).\n1229\t2. **SITUATION**: set the inning, half, outs, balls, strikes and score; runners on first, second and third; the\n1230\t   batter; the pitcher's stamina; both meters and turbo; the park; the wind. Then Apply.\n1231\t3. **CHEATS** (toggles):\n1232\t   - Batting and pitching: perfect contact always; every pitch a strike.\n1233\t   - Meters: infinite meter, infinite turbo.\n1234\t   - Play: fielders ignore walls (no clip); the CPU plays my side (autoplay); slow motion ×0.25; freeze the AI.\n1235\t   - Show: the zone always; the predicted pitch path and the timing bar; landing markers and AI targets; catch\n1236\t     radii and hitboxes.\n1237\t4. **TRIGGERS**:\n1238\t   - Force the next pitch (type and spot); force a home run on the next contact.\n1239\t   - Hazard now; meteor shower; Rhubarb; Batzooka cinematic; replay the last play.\n1240\t   - End the half-inning; walk-off now; win the game; lose the game.\n1241\t5. **WARP**:\n1242\t   - Scenes: title, attract, the gallery, the hub card (mocked), credits.\n1243\t   - Play: any Tour stop (pregame, game or postgame), any lesson, the Derby, an Exhibition in any park.\n1244\t   - Save: unlock all, +5,000 FB, reset the save.\n1245\t6. **INFO**:\n1246\t   - Live: fps, scene and phase, the ball's position and velocity, the last contact's numbers (timing ms, offset, Q,\n1247\t     EV, launch, spray), the seed, the input device and glyph family, the last 8 events, `E.versionLabel()`.\n1248\t   - **Frame step** (one 1/120 s step while frozen) and **Copy snapshot** (JSON to the clipboard).\n1249\t\n1250\t**The animation gallery** (from WARP) shows every new animation on rigs in turn, at normal speed or slowed, for review\n1251\twith `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n1252\t\n1253\t**Tests:** T-DEV-01..06 (the pause menu holds Dev Mode; a slider changes `TUNE` and the effect shows in the next pitch;\n1254\treset restores the defaults exactly; the situation applies; cheats toggle; warps land in their scene with no errors).\n1255\t\n1256\t### 6.24 Screens and UX flow (`34-menus.js`)\n1257\t\n1258\t**Principles:**\n1259\t- Big cards, never tables: at most 7 items to a screen.\n1260\t- The focus is obvious: it scales up 10%, gets a bright border and bobs gently.\n1261\t- A legend at the bottom shows glyph icons (\"✕ Select · ○ Back\").\n1262\t- Every screen works with the D-pad/stick, the keyboard (arrows/WASD, Enter/K, Escape/L) and the mouse (hover moves\n1263\t  the focus, click selects). Menus are pixel art drawn with `E.ui.box`, `E.font` and `E.font.title`.\n1264\t\n1265\t**Title:**\n1266\t- The background is the Kernel Field at dusk, seen from the Broadcast Cam, slowly panning, with fireflies; Dot\n1267\t  Dynamite stands with the Batzooka on her shoulder.\n1268\t- The logo \"BATZOOKA BASEBALL\" is drawn with `E.font.title` (scale 4, a gold-orange gradient, depth 3) and a rocket\n1269\t  trail sweeping under it.\n1270\t- Menu: CONTINUE (when there is a save), TOUR, EXHIBITION, DERBY, SPRING TRAINING, OPTIONS, ABOUT.\n1271\t- `E.versionLabel()` in a corner.\n1272\t\n1273\t**Team Select:**\n1274\t- Two big team cards (left away, right home): the captain's rig turning, the team name, three stat stars (Power,\n1275\t  Speed, Pitching), and the park they play in.\n1276\t- Left and right change the team; up and down move between the team, park, innings and difficulty rows.\n1277\t- The join row shows P1–P4 cards that slide to a side.\n1278\t\n1279\t**Results:**\n1280\t- The final score big; a one-line line score (R H E).\n1281\t- The game's **three stars** as cards (portrait, one line each).\n1282\t- FB earned, with a counting-up tween.\n1283\t- Buttons: Continue, Replay highlights, Rematch.\n1284\t\n1285\t**Playbook** (help, from the title's OPTIONS and the pause menu): one page per context (Batting, Pitching, Fielding,\n1286\tRunning, Supers, Rhubarb, House Rules). Each shows a picture of the action in the game's own art (a rig in the pose)\n1287\tand 3–5 big icon + verb lines for the current device.\n1288\t\n1289\t**About/Credits:** §6.27.\n1290\t\n1291\t**Tests:** T-UI-01..06 (every screen reachable and back; mouse hover moves the focus; clicks select; keyboard and pad\n1292\tnavigation; no screen over 7 items; the legend icons match the device).\n1293\t\n1294\t### 6.25 Settings and accessibility\n1295\t\n1296\tOurs are added with `menu: { settings: [...] }` / `settings.define`, beside the engine's own (volume, music, effects,\n1297\treduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, controls, dead zone, vibration):\n1298\t- **Gameplay:** Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / All-Star / Batzooka); Exhibition innings (1/3/5/9); Tour game length\n1299\t  (Standard / Quick); Mercy rule (On/Off); Comeback Boost (On/Off); Rhubarbs (On/Off); Derby length (2:00 / 10 outs).\n1300\t- **Assists:** Batting assist (Off / Timing hints / Big sweet spot); Pitching assist (Off / Auto-aim); Fielding\n1301\t  (Manual / Assisted / Auto); Baserunning (Manual / Auto).\n1302\t- **Display:** Timing feedback (On/Off); Strike zone (Always / During pitch / Off); Plate Cam (Classic / Close);\n1303\t  Field camera (Broadcast / High / Isometric); Replays (On / Big plays / Off).\n1304\t- **Sound:** Commentary (On / Big plays / Off); Voices (a 0–1 slider).\n1305\t- **Controller:** Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox), the engine's setting with our default (§3.3).\n1306\t- **Developer:** \"Keep dev tuning\" (On/Off).\n1307\t\n1308\tReduce motion also softens the camera cuts (fades instead of flashes) and turns off the screen shake in cinematics.\n1309\t\n1310\t### 6.26 Touch-only devices\n1311\t\n1312\t- **Detection, at boot:**\n1313\t  `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`.\n1314\t  It clears for good when a key is pressed or a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.padId` set).\n1315\t- **While touch-only:**\n1316\t  - The title shows a large card: a controller and a keyboard icon, \"Controller or keyboard required\", and a big\n1317\t    **Settings** button (it opens `game.menu('settings')`), beside the engine's corner menu button.\n1318\t  - There are no touch controls: `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, no scene has a `touch` list, and the engine's\n1319\t    touch-button settings stay hidden.\n1320\t  - The attract mode runs after 10 s as usual; a tap returns to the title card.\n1321\t  - Tapping menu items does nothing (the menus need a controller or keyboard), except the Settings button and the\n1322\t    corner button.\n1323\t\n1324\t**Tests:** T-TOUCH-01..03 (`#touch` forces the flag: the title shows the card, the menus ignore taps but Settings opens;\n1325\tthe attract mode starts after 10 s; a key press clears the flag).\n1326\t\n1327\t### 6.27 About/Credits, Source Information and the exit interview\n1328\t\n1329\t**The About/Credits screen** (from the title's ABOUT) has pages turned by\n1330\tL1/R1, left/right or clicks. Each page holds at most about 12 lines at scale 1, wrapped to the width.\n1331\t1. **ABOUT:** the logo; \"An original over-the-top arcade baseball game\"; the version (`E.versionLabel()`); the controls\n1332\t   in one line.\n1333\t2. **SOURCE INFORMATION**, every field TASK.md lists, filled in at the end of the build (\"unknown\" for anything that\n1334\t   can't be confirmed):\n1335\t   - Date: 2026-11-03 17:48:19.\n1336\t   - Source: baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style.\n1337\t   - Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n1338\t   - Genre and Perspective: §2.\n1339\t   - Tech: stack, engine and module versions, the changes made, mocap, the AI Hub client version, and Node and\n1340\t     Playwright for the tests.\n1341\t   - Art Direction, Animation, Audio: as built.\n1342\t   - Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a ghost-mode Derby round for 1–4 players, client 1.4.0.\n1343\t   - Testing: what was allowed and used.\n1344\t   - Research: what was read.\n1345\t   - Environment: \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\", or what is known.\n1346\t   - Model: as TASK.md gives it.\n1347\t   - Usage: estimates, or \"unknown\".\n1348\t3. **TESTING FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n1349\t4. **ENGINE FEEDBACK:** the six answers (including what changed in the engine, §3.3).\n1350\t5. **PROMPT FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n1351\t6. **CREDITS:**\n1352\t   - Design, code, art, music and writing: the model that built the game (named as in Source Information).\n1353\t   - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge).\n1354\t   - Motion: the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses); Quaternius and Mesh2Motion (CC0).\n1355\t   - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 (michaelcrosato/ai-hub).\n1356\t   - Any CC0 asset sets used (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG).\n1357\t   - \"Thanks for playing!\"\n1358\t\n1359\t**The same text** goes in the builder's final chat reply, and in `exit-interview.json` in exactly TASK.md's shape (§12).\n1360\tIts `aiHub` block should read:\n1361\t- `name`: \"Batzooka Baseball\"; `tagline`: \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\"\n1362\t- `description`: \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and\n1363\t  bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark.\"\n1364\t- `type`: \"Game\"; `genre`: \"Sports\"; `source`: \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\"; `series`: \"\"; `players`: \"1–4\".\n1365\t- `controls`: \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\".\n1366\t- `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"].\n1367\t\n1368\t---\n1369\t\n1370\t## 7. Content\n1371\t\n1372\tAll names, teams, parks and lines are original. Stats run 1–10. Batters have POW (power), CON (contact), SPD (speed),\n1373\tARM and GLV (glove); pitchers have VEL (velocity), CTL (control), BRK (break) and STA (stamina). Stat 5 is the ×1.0\n1374\tbaseline: a stat's factor is `1 + 0.03 · (stat − 5)` (0.88–1.15), unless a formula says otherwise. B/T is the side\n1375\tthey bat / throw from.\n1376\t\n1377\t### 7.1 Teams and rosters (`50-teams.js`)\n1378\t\n1379\t**1. Kernel Falls COMETS** (COM). The heroes: a scrappy farm-town team. Home: Kernel Field."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a605ca8e-6f21-4c44-bd74-b97a2b38e121","uuid":"4523988b-79ba-46ee-8a8b-ccc910f68f89","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:01:29.399Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/PLAN.md","content":"**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\nthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\nscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\nrestored).\n\n### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n\n**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n`game.cornerArea()`:\n- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n  pitcher's stamina bar.\n- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n  advance).\n- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n  the ball.\n- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n\n**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\nTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\ntoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n\n**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\nreturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n\n**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n\n**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\nfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\nthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n\n### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n\n**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\nbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\nThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. On the keyboard, the number keys and PC-standard keys come first (below), and\nthe I J K L cluster is an alternate set laid out like the face buttons.\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | Space, K | left button |\n| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q, Tab | – |\n| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n| `num1`–`num5` | (face buttons do these jobs) | | 1 2 3 4 5 | – |\n| pitch type | face buttons | | 1–5 (or K L J I, E) | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n\n**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n\n| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n\n- **The keyboard is PC-native first:** WASD and the mouse aim and run; Space swings and confirms; the mouse's left\n  button swings, throws at a clicked base and selects; the right button is the power swing; **number keys pick**\n  (`num1`–`num5`: 1–5 the pitch type when pitching, 1–4 the base, first to home, wherever a base is chosen); Shift\n  is turbo (sprint); Tab switches fielder; E is the super and \"go\"; Q is the steal or \"back\" modifier; C or Ctrl\n  dives; Escape pauses. The I J K L cluster stays as an alternate set that mirrors the face buttons, for players who\n  like a pad layout on keys. Keyboard prompts show the number keys first.\n- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n  use.\n- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n  setting).\n\n**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel and the number keys\npick pitch types, and the number keys pick bases; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\nof its contexts' prompts).\n\n### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n\n**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\nslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\ngusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n\n**The commentary director:**\n- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n\n**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n\n**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n    offset from a hash.\n  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n    10-ms noise burst.\n- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n- **Voice profiles:**\n\n| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |\n| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n\n- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n\n**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n\n**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\nno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n\n### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n\n**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\nno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\napart with a slow vibrato.\n\n| Song | Mood and makeup |\n|---|---|\n| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n| `training` | light and bouncy |\n| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n\n**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\nvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n\n**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n  engine's `explode`.\n- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n\n**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\nwarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n\n### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n\n- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n- **Behavior:**\n  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n  the between-innings shows.\n- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n\n**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\nhand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n\n### 6.18 Game modes\n\n- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n    reward item and the next pin.\n  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n  be replayed.\n\n### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n\n- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n\n### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n\n- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n- **Sides:**\n  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n  - With one human, they do everything.\n- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n\n**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\nplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n\n### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n\n**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\nsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\ntheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\nGhosts never affect your round.\n\nWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\ngame stays local (§6.20).\n\n**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/Space/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n```\nThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n\n**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n   normally to the title.\n3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n     check;\n   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n   \n   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()` (just 120000 when `limitMs` is missing or 0).\n6. **Reporting:**\n   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n     **once**.\n   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n   \n   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n   are canvas text, never HTML.\n\n**Session rules:**\n- **No title or restart:** Quit to title, Restart, Dev Mode and Pitching Change are hidden, and the back gesture is\n  swallowed.\n- **No popups or sockets:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, new windows or sockets.\n- **Storage:** the client already gives an empty storage, so add nothing for it.\n- **Pause:** the pause menu offers only Resume, Settings and Controls, and the round clock keeps running.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Headless T-HUB-01..08 use a mock `AIHub` in the vm context: connect, ready without input, the card's answers, start\n  at time 0, progress every second, a single result, time-up, end stopping input and sound, ghost messages at ≤ 20/s\n  and applied without changing the local score, and `Math.random` not called during the card.\n- Browser: the real harness in Playwright (§11.7).\n\n### 6.22 Attract mode (`35-attract.js`)\n\n- **Starts** after **10 s with no input** on the title (its mode menu included) or the other top-level menus (the Tour\n  menu, Team Select before anyone joins, Derby Setup, the lesson list, About). Input means any key, button, click,\n  touch, stick past the dead zone, or mouse movement over 4 px.\n- **Exits** on any input: back to the title (input consumed, so it doesn't pick a menu item); the idle timer restarts.\n- **What it is:** a looping **highlight reel played by the real game**, run by a director over `game.debug` with\n  cheats on (forced pitches and contact, infinite meter, no outs).\n- **On screen:** a \"DEMO PLAY\" banner and a pulsing \"PRESS ANY BUTTON\" (the icon for the device used last; on\n  touch-only devices \"TOUCH TO RETURN\"), plus a caption card between segments.\n- **Segments** (§7.11) cover the Plate Cam duel, super pitches, fielding, a diving catch, a Laser Arm throw, a steal and\n  slide, a bowl-over, a Batzooka moonshot with fireworks, a Rhubarb, low-gravity wall climbs, the Phantom Ball, the\n  drones and boss, the Derby with ghost comets, and the Moonshot Leaders table. One loop takes about 2.5 minutes.\n- **Robustness:** each segment has a time limit and moves on by itself if its event doesn't happen.\n- **Never** in an AI Hub session.\n\n**Tests:** T-ATT-01..05 (idle 10 s on the title starts it; it doesn't start during play or with the dev panel open;\nevery segment runs in order without errors and the reel loops; any input returns to the title; touch-only mode still\nruns it).\n\n### 6.23 Dev mode (`36-dev.js`)\n\n**Reached** from the pause menu's **Dev Mode** item (any pausable scene; hidden in hub sessions). It is a large,\n10-foot-readable panel over the frozen game, with six tabs. L1/R1 or Q/E change tabs; up and down move; left and right\nchange a value (hold to accelerate); ✕ toggles or activates; △ resets this value (or pins a slider); ○ closes. The\nmouse hovers, clicks, and drags sliders.\n\n1. **TUNE**: every `BZ.TUNE` value as a **live slider** grouped as in Appendix A (Pitching, Batting, Ball, Players,\n   Over the top, CPU, Camera, Game). Values apply instantly.\n   - **Reset to defaults** for all, and a reset per group.\n   - \"Keep tuning between visits\" stores the values in `E.store('bz:tune')`.\n   - **Pinned sliders:** △ pins up to 3. While playing, a thin strip at the top shows them; hold Create (Pad8) with\n     the D-pad to adjust (keyboard: `[` and `]` adjust, `\\` picks the next pin).\n2. **SITUATION**: set the inning, half, outs, balls, strikes and score; runners on first, second and third; the\n   batter; the pitcher's stamina; both meters and turbo; the park; the wind. Then Apply.\n3. **CHEATS** (toggles):\n   - Batting and pitching: perfect contact always; every pitch a strike.\n   - Meters: infinite meter, infinite turbo.\n   - Play: fielders ignore walls (no clip); the CPU plays my side (autoplay); slow motion ×0.25; freeze the AI.\n   - Show: the zone always; the predicted pitch path and the timing bar; landing markers and AI targets; catch\n     radii and hitboxes.\n4. **TRIGGERS**:\n   - Force the next pitch (type and spot); force a home run on the next contact.\n   - Hazard now; meteor shower; Rhubarb; Batzooka cinematic; replay the last play.\n   - End the half-inning; walk-off now; win the game; lose the game.\n5. **WARP**:\n   - Scenes: title, attract, the gallery, the hub card (mocked), credits.\n   - Play: any Tour stop (pregame, game or postgame), any lesson, the Derby, an Exhibition in any park.\n   - Save: unlock all, +5,000 FB, reset the save.\n6. **INFO**:\n   - Live: fps, scene and phase, the ball's position and velocity, the last contact's numbers (timing ms, offset, Q,\n     EV, launch, spray), the seed, the input device and glyph family, the last 8 events, `E.versionLabel()`.\n   - **Frame step** (one 1/120 s step while frozen) and **Copy snapshot** (JSON to the clipboard).\n\n**The animation gallery** (from WARP) shows every new animation on rigs in turn, at normal speed or slowed, for review\nwith `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n\n**Tests:** T-DEV-01..06 (the pause menu holds Dev Mode; a slider changes `TUNE` and the effect shows in the next pitch;\nreset restores the defaults exactly; the situation applies; cheats toggle; warps land in their scene with no errors).\n\n### 6.24 Screens and UX flow (`34-menus.js`)\n\n**Principles:**\n- Big cards, never tables: at most 7 items to a screen.\n- The focus is obvious: it scales up 10%, gets a bright border and bobs gently.\n- A legend at the bottom shows glyph icons (\"✕ Select · ○ Back\").\n- Every screen works with the D-pad/stick, the keyboard (arrows/WASD, Enter/K, Escape/L) and the mouse (hover moves\n  the focus, click selects). Menus are pixel art drawn with `E.ui.box`, `E.font` and `E.font.title`.\n\n**Title:**\n- The background is the Kernel Field at dusk, seen from the Broadcast Cam, slowly panning, with fireflies; Dot\n  Dynamite stands with the Batzooka on her shoulder.\n- The logo \"BATZOOKA BASEBALL\" is drawn with `E.font.title` (scale 4, a gold-orange gradient, depth 3) and a rocket\n  trail sweeping under it.\n- Menu: CONTINUE (when there is a save), TOUR, EXHIBITION, DERBY, SPRING TRAINING, OPTIONS, ABOUT.\n- `E.versionLabel()` in a corner.\n\n**Team Select:**\n- Two big team cards (left away, right home): the captain's rig turning, the team name, three stat stars (Power,\n  Speed, Pitching), and the park they play in.\n- Left and right change the team; up and down move between the team, park, innings and difficulty rows.\n- The join row shows P1–P4 cards that slide to a side.\n\n**Results:**\n- The final score big; a one-line line score (R H E).\n- The game's **three stars** as cards (portrait, one line each).\n- FB earned, with a counting-up tween.\n- Buttons: Continue, Replay highlights, Rematch.\n\n**Playbook** (help, from the title's OPTIONS and the pause menu): one page per context (Batting, Pitching, Fielding,\nRunning, Supers, Rhubarb, House Rules). Each shows a picture of the action in the game's own art (a rig in the pose)\nand 3–5 big icon + verb lines for the current device.\n\n**About/Credits:** §6.27.\n\n**Tests:** T-UI-01..06 (every screen reachable and back; mouse hover moves the focus; clicks select; keyboard and pad\nnavigation; no screen over 7 items; the legend icons match the device).\n\n### 6.25 Settings and accessibility\n\nOurs are added with `menu: { settings: [...] }` / `settings.define`, beside the engine's own (volume, music, effects,\nreduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, controls, dead zone, vibration):\n- **Gameplay:** Difficulty (Rookie / Pro / All-Star / Batzooka); Exhibition innings (1/3/5/9); Tour game length\n  (Standard / Quick); Mercy rule (On/Off); Comeback Boost (On/Off); Rhubarbs (On/Off); Derby length (2:00 / 10 outs).\n- **Assists:** Batting assist (Off / Timing hints / Big sweet spot); Pitching assist (Off / Auto-aim); Fielding\n  (Manual / Assisted / Auto); Baserunning (Manual / Auto).\n- **Display:** Timing feedback (On/Off); Strike zone (Always / During pitch / Off); Plate Cam (Classic / Close);\n  Field camera (Broadcast / High / Isometric); Replays (On / Big plays / Off).\n- **Sound:** Commentary (On / Big plays / Off); Voices (a 0–1 slider).\n- **Controller:** Button symbols (PlayStation / Xbox), the engine's setting with our default (§3.3).\n- **Developer:** \"Keep dev tuning\" (On/Off).\n\nReduce motion also softens the camera cuts (fades instead of flashes) and turns off the screen shake in cinematics.\n\n### 6.26 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Detection, at boot:**\n  `touchOnly = matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 0`.\n  It clears for good when a key is pressed or a gamepad connects (`gamepadconnected`, or `input.padId` set).\n- **While touch-only:**\n  - The title shows a large card: a controller and a keyboard icon, \"Controller or keyboard required\", and a big\n    **Settings** button (it opens `game.menu('settings')`), beside the engine's corner menu button.\n  - There are no touch controls: `game.input.touchEnabled = false`, no scene has a `touch` list, and the engine's\n    touch-button settings stay hidden.\n  - The attract mode runs after 10 s as usual; a tap returns to the title card.\n  - Tapping menu items does nothing (the menus need a controller or keyboard), except the Settings button and the\n    corner button.\n\n**Tests:** T-TOUCH-01..03 (`#touch` forces the flag: the title shows the card, the menus ignore taps but Settings opens;\nthe attract mode starts after 10 s; a key press clears the flag).\n\n### 6.27 About/Credits, Source Information and the exit interview\n\n**The About/Credits screen** (from the title's ABOUT) has pages turned by\nL1/R1, left/right or clicks. Each page holds at most about 12 lines at scale 1, wrapped to the width.\n1. **ABOUT:** the logo; \"An original over-the-top arcade baseball game\"; the version (`E.versionLabel()`); the controls\n   in one line.\n2. **SOURCE INFORMATION**, every field TASK.md lists, filled in at the end of the build (\"unknown\" for anything that\n   can't be confirmed):\n   - Date: 2026-11-03 17:48:19.\n   - Source: baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style.\n   - Plan: `PLAN.md`.\n   - Genre and Perspective: §2.\n   - Tech: stack, engine and module versions, the changes made, mocap, the AI Hub client version, and Node and\n     Playwright for the tests.\n   - Art Direction, Animation, Audio: as built.\n   - Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1, a ghost-mode Derby round for 1–4 players, client 1.4.0.\n   - Testing: what was allowed and used.\n   - Research: what was read.\n   - Environment: \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\", or what is known.\n   - Model: as TASK.md gives it.\n   - Usage: estimates, or \"unknown\".\n3. **TESTING FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n4. **ENGINE FEEDBACK:** the six answers (including what changed in the engine, §3.3).\n5. **PROMPT FEEDBACK:** the four answers.\n6. **CREDITS:**\n   - Design, code, art, music and writing: the model that built the game (named as in Source Information).\n   - Engine: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (MIT, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge).\n   - Motion: the CMU Graphics Lab Motion Capture Database (free for all uses); Quaternius and Mesh2Motion (CC0).\n   - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 (michaelcrosato/ai-hub).\n   - Any CC0 asset sets used (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG).\n   - \"Thanks for playing!\"\n\n**The same text** goes in the builder's final chat reply, and in `exit-interview.json` in exactly TASK.md's shape (§12).\nIts `aiHub` block should read:\n- `name`: \"Batzooka Baseball\"; `tagline`: \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\"\n- `description`: \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and\n  bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark.\"\n- `type`: \"Game\"; `genre`: \"Sports\"; `source`: \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\"; `series`: \"\"; `players`: \"1–4\".\n- `controls`: \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\".\n- `tags`: [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"].\n\n---\n\n## 7. Content\n\nAll names, teams, parks and lines are original. Stats run 1–10. Batters have POW (power), CON (contact), SPD (speed),\nARM and GLV (glove); pitchers have VEL (velocity), CTL (control), BRK (break) and STA (stamina). Stat 5 is the ×1.0\nbaseline: a stat's factor is `1 + 0.03 · (stat − 5)` (0.88–1.15), unless a formula says otherwise. B/T is the side\nthey bat / throw from.\n\n### 7.1 Teams and rosters (`50-teams.js`)\n\n**1. Kernel Falls COMETS** (COM). The heroes: a scrappy farm-town team. Home: Kernel Field.","numLines":560,"startLine":820,"totalLines":2144}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01ApLao1XAw5E2dUgL2BKNK9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
