{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi2SpQ7RMH8bage9hXnA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JRDZz2KcxqZm45ccVG3x6N","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-060114/about.json","content":"{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-04 06:01:14\",\n    \"source\": \"Baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style\",\n    \"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n    \"genre\": \"Arcade sports (baseball), with a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light team upgrades\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Plate Cam: a Mode 7 pseudo-3D view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing; Broadcast Cam: a three-quarter ground view of the park for play in the field (High and Isometric options); Moonshot Cam: a Mode 7 chase camera on home runs\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine (the full engine with its Mode 7 module; five small changes listed in ENGINE-CHANGES.md); vanilla JavaScript in one IIFE built from 23 source files; AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 pasted unmodified; one self-contained HTML file with no CDN or network requests; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for the tests\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at res ps1 (expanded to fill the screen), six parks painted from code (mowing patterns, chalk, dirt, warning tracks, cornfields, a bay, lava, regolith, graveyards, neon), Backdrop skies, code-drawn ballpark props, crowds of about 900 fans per park, night lighting and particles\",\n    \"animation\": \"The engine's procedural Humanoid rig system (its attack specs, poses and gait), with new specs for the swing, the bunt, the pitch delivery and the umpire's calls; E.Combo punches, shoves and tosses for the Rhubarb; E.Blob mascots, gulls and drones; no motion-capture clips\",\n    \"audio\": \"The engine's chip synth only: 14 original songs (a ballpark-organ tune per park with hype layers, title, menus, Tour map, Derby, Rhubarb, training, credits, hub card), 7 stings, about 60 sound effects, and Animalese-style babble voices for 16 characters (one syllable per letter, with subtitles)\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players: an online Batzooka Derby round with the game's own card, ready with no key press, progress every second, one result, time-up handling, and other players shown as ghost batters and home-run comets; local multiplayer for 1-4 on one machine\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless as allowed: one command (node tests/run.mjs, 102 checks across 12 suites, including a scripted playthrough of the Tour, all eight lessons, a four-player Exhibition, a hot-seat Derby and the About pages by an input-driven bot), seeded CPU-vs-CPU balance sims, the engine's check.mjs in Chromium, Playwright screenshots for the look, and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host leave) served on 127.0.0.1 with an OS-picked port\",\n    \"research\": \"Read the engine's API card, API index, AI guide and asset card, the sports and kart kits, the Mode 7 module's header, and the AI Hub README; no other research\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (tests ran in Playwright Chromium)\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\n    \"usage\": \"Cost and token counts unknown (no usage meter in the session); session time about 1 hour 20 minutes by the machine clock (06:01 to about 07:20)\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"About 28 issues: 12 code bugs (two syntax errors, two NaN pitch speeds that froze games, a crash when fielders were missing, a bunt flag that never reset, late swings being called strikes before they could connect, a pitch type with no aim, absurd home-run distances, a wrong deep link pairing a team with itself, a long commentary line, a story scene that skipped itself headless), a badly unbalanced first version (.600 batting average), and about 15 layout problems found in screenshots (overflowing menus, missing font glyphs, the catcher filling the Plate Cam, overlapping HUD pieces).\",\n    \"6 were game-breaking: the two syntax errors (nothing loaded), the two NaN pitches (the game froze mid-pitch), the crash on contact with no fielders set, and the late-swing bug (a swing inside the timing window but after the plate crossing could never make contact).\",\n    \"The seeded CPU-vs-CPU simulations: they found both NaN freezes and the stuck bunt flag, and gave the numbers (runs, K%, HR%, average) used to balance the game; second was the bot-played Derby, which exposed the late-swing ordering bug.\",\n    \"The engine's check.mjs sweep over the deep links: it reported no errors, but by then the headless suites and scenario screenshots had already covered the same pages, and its random key presses left the snapshots in odd states.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"The Mode 7 module: a Plate Cam from behind the catcher, with the park's own floor, a turning Backdrop sky and rigs as distance-scaled billboards, took a few dozen lines. Close behind: headless fixed-step advance with input.tap and hold, which made bot-driven playthrough tests easy.\",\n    \"A few wrong guesses: menu.settings must be an array when the game is constructed (I passed a function); E.Dialog has no width or position option that fits a TV HUD, so I wrote my own radio box; a // comment I appended mid-line in the engine broke the build; promises from a mock in a headless test needed an await.\",\n    \"The baseball itself: a parametric pitch flight with a known plate crossing, the contact model (timing and reticle offset to spray, launch and exit speed), batted-ball physics with the fence, poles and foul territory, fielders assigned by intercept time with covers and throws, baserunners racing the ball, and the rules engine (forces, tags, tag-ups, the third-out rule, walk-offs, mercy, extra innings).\",\n    \"Drawing the ballpark's vertical parts in Mode 7: the fence, the foul walls and the stands had to be hand-projected quads gathered as billboards, because Mode 7 has a floor and billboards but no walls.\",\n    \"Five small changes, all marked BATZOOKA: a 'bat' weapon for Humanoid (rest pose, tip, HD and classic drawing); a 'mitt' option drawn on the glove hand; the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ ■ ● — added to the 5x7 font; the Button symbols setting defaulting to PlayStation with an Xbox choice; and a fix to my own broken comment.\",\n    \"A Mode 7 wall primitive (for example m7.quad or m7.wall) that projects and depth-sorts world-space polygons with the billboards, so stadium walls, stands and buildings need no hand-built quads.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The requirements are concrete and checkable: 10 seconds to the attract mode, dev sliders with a reset, PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, a touch-only card, and an online protocol with a real harness to pass.\",\n    \"PLAN.md's deliverable names (claude-opus-5-5-max with another timestamp) disagree with TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, so the files use TASK.md's names. It was also not clear how much of the plan's very large scope is required versus aspirational.\",\n    \"Scope: the plan describes six teams, six parks, a Tour with cutscenes, eight lessons, a Derby, the online round, attract and dev modes and mocap animation, all in one session; choosing what to build fully and testing all of it was the hard part.\",\n    \"Say which plan items are required and which are optional (or give a time budget), and keep the plan's file names in step with TASK.md's.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Batzooka Baseball\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\",\n    \"description\": \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark. Online, it's a two-minute home-run derby against the ghosts of your friends.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Sports\",\n    \"source\": \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\",\n    \"tags\": [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"]\n  }\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3132,"cache_read_input_tokens":625498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3132},"output_tokens":60,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a605ca8e-6f21-4c44-bd74-b97a2b38e121","uuid":"718c6c1a-f49e-4352-92f4-16925a216bc4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:13:41.548Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi2Sp4mNg1wMzUPQYbbH","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01JRDZz2KcxqZm45ccVG3x6N":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-060114__x4549c1/work/BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-060114/about.json","content":"{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-04 06:01:14\",\n    \"source\": \"Baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style\",\n    \"plan\": \"PLAN.md\",\n    \"genre\": \"Arcade sports (baseball), with a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light team upgrades\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Plate Cam: a Mode 7 pseudo-3D view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing; Broadcast Cam: a three-quarter ground view of the park for play in the field (High and Isometric options); Moonshot Cam: a Mode 7 chase camera on home runs\",\n    \"tech\": \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine (the full engine with its Mode 7 module; five small changes listed in ENGINE-CHANGES.md); vanilla JavaScript in one IIFE built from 23 source files; AI Hub Session client 1.4.0 pasted unmodified; one self-contained HTML file with no CDN or network requests; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for the tests\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at res ps1 (expanded to fill the screen), six parks painted from code (mowing patterns, chalk, dirt, warning tracks, cornfields, a bay, lava, regolith, graveyards, neon), Backdrop skies, code-drawn ballpark props, crowds of about 900 fans per park, night lighting and particles\",\n    \"animation\": \"The engine's procedural Humanoid rig system (its attack specs, poses and gait), with new specs for the swing, the bunt, the pitch delivery and the umpire's calls; E.Combo punches, shoves and tosses for the Rhubarb; E.Blob mascots, gulls and drones; no motion-capture clips\",\n    \"audio\": \"The engine's chip synth only: 14 original songs (a ballpark-organ tune per park with hype layers, title, menus, Tour map, Derby, Rhubarb, training, credits, hub card), 7 stings, about 60 sound effects, and Animalese-style babble voices for 16 characters (one syllable per letter, with subtitles)\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players: an online Batzooka Derby round with the game's own card, ready with no key press, progress every second, one result, time-up handling, and other players shown as ghost batters and home-run comets; local multiplayer for 1-4 on one machine\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless as allowed: one command (node tests/run.mjs, 102 checks across 12 suites, including a scripted playthrough of the Tour, all eight lessons, a four-player Exhibition, a hot-seat Derby and the About pages by an input-driven bot), seeded CPU-vs-CPU balance sims, the engine's check.mjs in Chromium, Playwright screenshots for the look, and the real AI Hub harness (2 players; 4 players with latency, jitter and loss; host leave) served on 127.0.0.1 with an OS-picked port\",\n    \"research\": \"Read the engine's API card, API index, AI guide and asset card, the sports and kart kits, the Mode 7 module's header, and the AI Hub README; no other research\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (tests ran in Playwright Chromium)\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\",\n    \"usage\": \"Cost and token counts unknown (no usage meter in the session); session time about 1 hour 20 minutes by the machine clock (06:01 to about 07:20)\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"About 28 issues: 12 code bugs (two syntax errors, two NaN pitch speeds that froze games, a crash when fielders were missing, a bunt flag that never reset, late swings being called strikes before they could connect, a pitch type with no aim, absurd home-run distances, a wrong deep link pairing a team with itself, a long commentary line, a story scene that skipped itself headless), a badly unbalanced first version (.600 batting average), and about 15 layout problems found in screenshots (overflowing menus, missing font glyphs, the catcher filling the Plate Cam, overlapping HUD pieces).\",\n    \"6 were game-breaking: the two syntax errors (nothing loaded), the two NaN pitches (the game froze mid-pitch), the crash on contact with no fielders set, and the late-swing bug (a swing inside the timing window but after the plate crossing could never make contact).\",\n    \"The seeded CPU-vs-CPU simulations: they found both NaN freezes and the stuck bunt flag, and gave the numbers (runs, K%, HR%, average) used to balance the game; second was the bot-played Derby, which exposed the late-swing ordering bug.\",\n    \"The engine's check.mjs sweep over the deep links: it reported no errors, but by then the headless suites and scenario screenshots had already covered the same pages, and its random key presses left the snapshots in odd states.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"The Mode 7 module: a Plate Cam from behind the catcher, with the park's own floor, a turning Backdrop sky and rigs as distance-scaled billboards, took a few dozen lines. Close behind: headless fixed-step advance with input.tap and hold, which made bot-driven playthrough tests easy.\",\n    \"A few wrong guesses: menu.settings must be an array when the game is constructed (I passed a function); E.Dialog has no width or position option that fits a TV HUD, so I wrote my own radio box; a // comment I appended mid-line in the engine broke the build; promises from a mock in a headless test needed an await.\",\n    \"The baseball itself: a parametric pitch flight with a known plate crossing, the contact model (timing and reticle offset to spray, launch and exit speed), batted-ball physics with the fence, poles and foul territory, fielders assigned by intercept time with covers and throws, baserunners racing the ball, and the rules engine (forces, tags, tag-ups, the third-out rule, walk-offs, mercy, extra innings).\",\n    \"Drawing the ballpark's vertical parts in Mode 7: the fence, the foul walls and the stands had to be hand-projected quads gathered as billboards, because Mode 7 has a floor and billboards but no walls.\",\n    \"Five small changes, all marked BATZOOKA: a 'bat' weapon for Humanoid (rest pose, tip, HD and classic drawing); a 'mitt' option drawn on the glove hand; the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ ■ ● — added to the 5x7 font; the Button symbols setting defaulting to PlayStation with an Xbox choice; and a fix to my own broken comment.\",\n    \"A Mode 7 wall primitive (for example m7.quad or m7.wall) that projects and depth-sorts world-space polygons with the billboards, so stadium walls, stands and buildings need no hand-built quads.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"The requirements are concrete and checkable: 10 seconds to the attract mode, dev sliders with a reset, PlayStation glyphs by default with an Xbox option, a touch-only card, and an online protocol with a real harness to pass.\",\n    \"PLAN.md's deliverable names (claude-opus-5-5-max with another timestamp) disagree with TASK.md's; TASK.md wins, so the files use TASK.md's names. It was also not clear how much of the plan's very large scope is required versus aspirational.\",\n    \"Scope: the plan describes six teams, six parks, a Tour with cutscenes, eight lessons, a Derby, the online round, attract and dev modes and mocap animation, all in one session; choosing what to build fully and testing all of it was the hard part.\",\n    \"Say which plan items are required and which are optional (or give a time budget), and keep the plan's file names in step with TASK.md's.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"Batzooka Baseball\",\n    \"tagline\": \"Swing for the moon. Literally.\",\n    \"description\": \"Over-the-top arcade baseball: fireball pitches, a rocket-powered bat, wall-climbing catches and bench-clearing rhubarbs on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark. Online, it's a two-minute home-run derby against the ghosts of your friends.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Sports\",\n    \"source\": \"Baseball (over-the-top arcade style)\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Stick/WASD/mouse aim · ✕/Space/click swing · □/J/right-click power · R1/E Batzooka\",\n    \"tags\": [\"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"versus\", \"pixel art\", \"boss fights\", \"retro\"]\n  }\n}\n"}}}
