{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi3BRZsyu75W1NgkFWg5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've added pickoffs and big-play replays, so nothing from the earlier limitations list is left. The full suite now runs 106 checks and all pass. The browser checker shows no errors, and the AI Hub harness passed all three scenarios again.\n\n**What I added this round**\n- **Pickoffs:** before a pitch, the pitcher holds L1 and presses a base button to throw over. The offense can press R1 to take an extra lead when the Batzooka isn't ready; a runner with an extra lead is easier to pick off. CPU pitchers sometimes try it on fast runners. It is shown in the on-screen prompts and the Playbook, and I removed \"no pickoffs\" from the House Rules.\n- **Replays:** after a home run, a diving or wall catch, a double play or a collision, the play reruns at half speed with a REPLAY banner. You can skip it, and there is a Replays setting to turn it off.\n\n**On the other two points**\n- **File name and place:** the game is `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-060114.html`, which is the name TASK.md asks for. It sits in this folder's root, next to `exit-interview.json`; that folder is also the parent of the dev folder. PLAN.md's \"max\" names were wrong, and TASK.md says its own rules win.\n- **Motion capture and \"unknown\":** motion capture was only a PLAN.md suggestion. TASK.md asks for the engine's own animation system, which the game uses. Usage cost and tokens say \"unknown\" because TASK.md asks for that when something can't be confirmed.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:01:14\n- **Source:** Baseball (the sport), in the over-the-top arcade style\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:**\n  - Genre: arcade sports (baseball), with a slapstick brawl mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light team upgrades.\n  - Perspective: pitches and swings are seen from behind the catcher in a pseudo-3D (Mode 7) view. Play in the field uses a three-quarter view, with High and Isometric options. Home runs get a chase camera. Replays show in the field view.\n- **Tech:**\n  - my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff engine: the full engine with its Mode 7 module, plus five small changes listed in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`.\n  - Plain JavaScript built from 28 source files into one script.\n  - AI Hub Session client 1.4.0, pasted in unmodified.\n  - One self-contained HTML file with no CDN or network requests.\n  - Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 (Chromium) for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's own pixel-art look. Characters are the engine's rigs drawn as pixel art at PS1 resolution. Six parks are painted from code, with code-drawn skies and props, about 900 fans each, night lighting and particles.\n- **Animation:** the engine's procedural rig system, with new motions for the swing, bunt, pitch delivery and umpire calls. Rhubarb punches, shoves and tosses; blob-style mascots, gulls and drones. No motion-capture clips.\n- **Audio:** the engine's chip synth only: 14 original songs, 7 stings, 45 sound effects, and Animalese-style babble voices for 16 characters.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode, 1–4 players. Each player bats an online Derby round, and the others appear as ghost batters and home-run comets. Local multiplayer for 1–4 players on one machine.\n- **Testing:** headless, as allowed:\n  - One command, `node tests/run.mjs`: 106 checks in 13 suites, including a scripted playthrough by a bot that uses the real controls.\n  - Seeded CPU-vs-CPU balance simulations.\n  - The engine's `check.mjs` browser checker.\n  - Playwright screenshots, used only for the look.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in three scenarios, served on 127.0.0.1 with a port the OS picks.\n- **Research:** read the engine's API card, API index, guide and asset card, its sports and kart example games, the Mode 7 module's header, and the AI Hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown (the tests ran in Playwright Chromium)\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** cost and token counts unknown. Session time about 1.5 hours by the machine clock.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 28:\n   - 12 code bugs, including two syntax errors, two pitch speeds that came out as NaN and froze the game, a crash on contact, a bunt flag that never reset, and late swings being called strikes before they could connect.\n   - A badly unbalanced first build: the CPU batted around .600.\n   - About 15 layout problems found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical or game-breaking?** 6: the two syntax errors, the two NaN pitch freezes, the crash on contact, and the late-swing bug.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the seeded CPU-vs-CPU simulations. They found both freezes and the stuck bunt flag, and gave the numbers I balanced the game with. Second was the bot-played Derby, which exposed the late-swing bug.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the engine's `check.mjs` sweep. It found nothing new, and its random key presses left the game in odd states.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The Mode 7 module, which made the view from behind the catcher simple to build. Also headless fixed-step `advance()` with simulated taps and holds, which made the bot playthrough tests easy.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n   - `menu.settings` must be an array when the game is created.\n   - The engine's dialog box can't be sized or placed to fit the HUD, so I wrote my own commentary box.\n   - A comment I added in the middle of an engine line broke the build.\n   - Mock promises in headless tests needed an `await`.\n3. **Most important thing you built yourself:** the baseball itself:\n   - pitch flight;\n   - the bat-to-ball contact model;\n   - batted-ball physics around the fence, poles and foul territory;\n   - fielders choosing who chases and who covers which base, and throwing;\n   - baserunners racing the ball;\n   - the rules engine, pickoffs and replays.\n4. **Hardest part of using the engine:** drawing the fence, foul walls and stands in Mode 7. It has a floor and flat billboards but no walls, so each wall section had to be projected by hand.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - Added a `'bat'` weapon so batters can swing with the engine's attack system.\n   - Added a `mitt` option so fielders read as baseball players.\n   - Added ✕ ○ □ △ · ✓ ■ ● — to the 5×7 font, because the PlayStation button names drew as \"?\".\n   - Made PlayStation the default Button symbols setting, with an Xbox choice, as TASK.md asks.\n   - Fixed the comment I broke myself.\n6. **One thing you'd change:** add a Mode 7 wall primitive that projects and depth-sorts world-space polygons.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The requirements are concrete and checkable, and the online protocol comes with a real harness to pass.\n2. **What was confusing?** PLAN.md's file names (\"max\", a different timestamp) disagree with TASK.md's, and it wasn't clear how much of the plan's very large scope was required.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Scope: building the core game fully across every mode and testing all of it in one session.\n4. **What would you change?** Mark which plan items are required and which are optional, and keep the plan's file names in step with TASK.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":806,"cache_read_input_tokens":650840,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":806},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a605ca8e-6f21-4c44-bd74-b97a2b38e121","uuid":"2a698145-f6c6-4fd2-8705-a65562f14f61","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:23:14.411Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi3BR6LwgrZZsdNw8Xw2"}
